  designNotes: 'Beat 11: the exam. The chart offers a helicopter here OR a focus-fire
    test; HELI_ENABLED is false for camera-load reasons that are a real problem rather
    than a stale flag, so this is the skill version. Rocket troopers and heavies
    on one post: everything is either fragile-and-dangerous or tough-and-slow, and
    the player has to sort them under a turn limit of their own patience. Was a 6v6
    stub with no payout that broke the locked 7-30 roster scale. Wave added 2026-08-07
    (Tier 1.2 schedule). This was the only stage-2 level the audit called NO MECHANIC,
    and the beat chart names its own fallback - "else elite push" - so the push is
    delivered as a TELEGRAPHED wave rather than as three more bodies in the opening
    formation. Three heavies on turn 4, warned from turn 2, is what turns the focus-fire
    exam into a timed one: sort the rockets from the heavies BEFORE the reinforcement
    lands, or sort a bigger problem afterwards. The lead is 2 to match L10, so the
    campaign teaches one clock, not two. Ground units at x 8, NOT garrisoned: the
    beach post is already at 288-capacity limits and a wave that had to be shot off
    a deck would spend siege damage the squad does not have. Waves are enemy-side
    only, so the star math (player-roster based) is untouched. WAVE MOVED 2026-08-12, and this one CHANGED THE BEAT rather than just the
    geometry: its three heavies used to arrive at anchorX 8, which put the first of them 0.71
    INSIDE GarrisonPost's collision box - unhittable except by a near-vertical plunge (rule 8,
    which until that day never looked at mid-battle arrivals). Behind the post there is no room
    to fix it: the window between the box edge (7.88) and the back rank's flat reach (9.66) is
    1.78 wide and the three-man group spans 1.65, leaving ~0.1 of margin against formation
    jitter, which is a coin flip and not a fix. So the wave now lands IN FRONT of the post at
    anchorX 0. The elite push is still a push and still telegraphed two turns out, but it now
    arrives between the player and the post rather than behind it - closer, hittable
    immediately, and killed sooner. If that reads as too soft on a device, the alternatives are
    a two-man wave behind the post (which fits the window) or accepting a back-rank reach
    warning of the kind L3 and L5 already carry.'
